{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "תריבון", :translit "tarivun", :gloss "you [plural] dispute"} {:he "מה תריבון עמדי", :translit "mah tarivun imadi", :gloss "Why do you dispute with me"} {:he "האתם תריבון לבעל", :translit "atem tarivun la-ba'al", :gloss "Will you dispute for the Ba'al"} {:he "אם לאל תריבון", :translit "im la-el tarivun", :gloss "If it is to God you dispute"} {:he "אם תריבון לבעל", :translit "im tarivun la-ba'al", :gloss "if you dispute for the Ba'al"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מה תריבון עמדי" "האתם תריבון לבעל" "אם לאל תריבון"]} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay pivoting on 'ba'al' as both a divine title for God and a term for a master/teacher"}], :frame {:text "The principle that disputing with one's rabbi is akin to disputing with God is articulated in multiple Talmudic passages. Sanhedrin 110a derives from Numbers 17:25 that one who quarrels with his teacher is as one who quarrels with the Shekhinah. Eruvin 63b addresses the severity of ruling in front of one's teacher, and Sotah 40b records that Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah expounded a baraita in the presence of his teacher Rabban Gamliel only after being separated, illustrating deference to a master's authority. The verse at Exodus 17:2 is read as exploiting the word 'ba'al,' which can denote a master or husband (as in Proverbs 31:11, where the wife's husband is called her ba'al), yet also designates the Canaanite deity; thus the same linguistic root can collapse disputing a human teacher into the service of a false god.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 110a" "Eruvin 63b" "Sotah 40b" "Exodus 17:2" "Numbers 17:25" "Proverbs 31:11"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מה תריבון עמדי" "האתם תריבון לבעל" "אם לאל תריבון"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "מה תריבון עמדי", :refs ["Exodus 17:2"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "האתם תריבון לבעל", :refs ["Judges 6:31"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "אם לאל תריבון", :refs ["Job 13:8"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay pivoting on 'ba'al' as both a divine title for God and a term for a master/teacher", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:16:52.228865252Z", :model "glm-5.2"}